===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰel-|id=blow}}
From {{inh|en|enm|bole}}, {{m|enm|bul}}, {{m|enm|bule}}, from a conflation of {{der|en|ang|bula||bull, steer}} and {{der|en|non|boli}}, both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*bulô||bull}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰl̥no-}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*bʰel-||to blow, swell up}}. Cognate with {{cog|fy|bolle}}, {{cog|nl|bul}}, {{cog|nds-de|Bull}}, {{cog|de|Bulle}}, {{cog|sv|bulla}}; also {{cog|sga|ball||limb}}, {{cog|la|follis||bellows, leather bag}}, {{cog|txh|βόλινθος||wild bull|sc=Grek}}, [[Slovene|Serbo-Croatian]] and [[Slovene]] ''[[vol]]'' ("ox"), {{cog|sq|buall|t=buffalo}} or related {{m|sq|bolle||testicles}}, {{cog|grc|φαλλός||penis}}.

====Noun====
[[File:Toro de lidia monument, Tordesillas, Valladolid, Spain - 20050630.jpg|thumb|A statue of a [[w:Spanish Fighting Bull|Spanish fighting bull]] or ''toro de lidia'' in [[w:Tordesillas|Tordesillas]], [[w:Valladolid|Valladolid]], Spain]]
{{en-noun|~}}

# An adult male of domesticated [[cattle]] or [[ox]]en.
## Specifically, one that is uncastrated.
# A male of domesticated [[cattle]] or [[ox]]en of any age.
# Any adult male [[bovine]].
# An adult male of certain large mammals, such as [[whale]]s, [[elephant]]s, [[camel]]s and [[seal]]s.<!--do not put "etc" here: these are examples, not an exhaustive list-->
# A large, strong man.
# {{lb|en|finance}} An [[investor]] who buys (commodities or securities) in anticipation of a rise in prices.
#* '''1821''', Bank of England, ''The Bank - The Stock Exchange - The Bankers ...'' (page 64)
#*: This accompt has been made to appear a '''bull''' accompt, ''i.e.'' that the '''bulls''' cannot take their stock. The fact is the reverse; it is a bear accompt, but the bears, unable to deliver their stock, have conjointly banged the market, and pocketed the tickets, to defeat the rise and loss that would have ensued to them by their buying on a rising price on the accompt day {{...}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[policeman]]. <!--where is this used?-->
#* {{RQ:Rinehart Hopwood Bat|I|01|passage=The Bat—they called him the Bat.{{nb...}}. He'd never been in stir, the '''bulls''' had never mugged him, he didn't run with a mob, he played a lone hand, and fenced his stuff so that even the fence couldn't swear he knew his face.}}
## {{lb|en|US}} Specifically, a [[policeman]] employed in a [[railroad]] [[yard]].
# {{lb|en|LGBT|slang}} An elderly lesbian.<ref name="Niemoeller"/>
# {{lb|en|UK|historical|obsolete|_|slang}} A [[crown]] [[coin]]; its [[value]], {{nowrap|5 [[shilling]]s.}}
#* '''1859''', {{w|J.C. Hotten}}, ''{{w|A Dictionary of Modern Slang, Cant, and Vulgar Words}}''
#*: ''[[half-a-crown|Half-a-crown]]'' is known as an {{smallcaps|[[alderman]]}}, {{smallcaps|half a [[bull]]}}, {{smallcaps|half a [[tusheroon]]}}, and a {{smallcaps|[[madza caroon]]}}; [[whilst]] a ''[[crown]]'' piece, or ''five [[shilling]]s'', may be called either a {{smallcaps|[[bull]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[caroon]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[cartwheel]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[coachwheel]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[thick-un]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[tusheroon]]}}.
# {{lb|en|UK}} {{clipping of|en|bullseye}}
#* '''1926''', {{w|T.E. Lawrence}}, ''Seven Pillars of Wisdom'', New York: Anchor (1991), p. 219:
#*: A second good game was to cannon one galloping camel with another, and crash it into a near tree. Either the tree went down (valley trees in the light Hejaz soil were notably unstable things) or the rider was scratched and torn; or, best of all, he was swept quite out of his saddle, and left impaled on a thorny branch, if not dropped violently to the ground. This counted as a '''bull''', and was very popular with everyone but him.
## {{lb|en|military|firearms}} The central portion of a [[target]], inside the [[inner]] and [[magpie]].
# {{lb|en|Philadelphia|slang}} A [[man]] or [[boy]] (derived from the Philadelphia English pronunciation of “boy”, which is practically a homophone of “bull”)
# {{lb|en|uncountable|informal|euphemistic|slang}} {{clipping of|en|bullshit}}
# A man who has sex with another man's wife or girlfriend with the consent of both.
#* '''2018''' [https://www.thesun.co.uk/fabulous/6432973/stag-men-love-watching-other-guys-have-sex-with-their-wives-but-its-not-cuckolding/ ‘Stag’ men love watching other guys have sex with their wives… but it’s not cuckolding]
#*: The Vixen, often known as ‘Hotwife’, has sex with the encouragement of her husband or boyfriend with the '''Bull''' (that’s the guy who is servicing her). Another scenario is that the Vixen has sex with a '''Bull''' outside of the couple’s shared abode. Then she comes home and recounts all the details in a blow-by-blow description to turn the Stag on.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A drink made by pouring water into a cask that previously held liquor.
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} {{1|beef}}.
#* '''1949''', Stephen Peter Llewellyn, ''Journey Towards Christmas'' (page 142)
#*: Meanwhile the Tommies had discovered several large tins of ham in the captured lorry. 'That,' said the big Nazi, 'is for our tea.' 'No,' said a Tommy sergeant-major. 'That's for ''our'' tea. For you, chummy, we've kept a nice bit of '''bull'''.'

